. * ============================
. * Analysis
. * ============================
. 
. * FILTERS * (Please adapt according to your needs)
.  
.         * only those with children; sumkids > 0 
.         drop if num_children <= 0
(536 observations deleted)

.         
.         * drop if missing values; use gender as main indicator
.         drop if missing(gender)
(322 observations deleted)

.         
.         * drop negative values for wage
.         drop if income < 0
(392 observations deleted)

.         
.         * drop if negative values for emplyoment
.         drop if employment < 0 
(0 observations deleted)

.         
.         * drop if negative values for marital_status
.         drop if marital_status < 0 
(0 observations deleted)

.         
.         * drop those with no answer for political affiliation
.         drop if pol_affiliation == -1
(4 observations deleted)

.         
. * GENERATE VARIABLES *
. 
. 
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H1 *
.         
.         * Description:
.         * We assume that nature randomly assigns the gender of children and b
> ased on the subset
.         * of people who became parents (unobserved variables might drive the 
> decision to become a parent
.         * in the first place), we estimate the effect of one additional daugh
> ter on a question related
.         * to gender equity attitudes elicited in 2018 (relate to shared respo
> nsibility within households;
.         * likert scale item from 1 - 7 with higher values indicating higher a
> ttitudes towards gender equity)
.         * Additionally, we include control variables used in similar papers i
> n the past. Finally,
.         * we use heteroskedastic robust standard errors. 
. 
.         reg plh0308_v2 num_daughters i.num_children ib2.gender age age_sqrd i
> ncome i.employment i.marital_status i.pol_affiliation i.higher_education, rob
> ust

